{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Do not turn.</b> It says [this] in the language of turning, to prohibit the thought of idolatrous worship — that one should not turn in his mind to contemplate it. And to give you insight into the matter of truth, know that the aspect of אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים (other gods) is associated with the aspect of אֲחוֹרַיִם (the back), and when a person contemplates it, they turn that side toward פָּנִים (the front), and the one who understands will comprehend." "<b>The elilim.</b> It says [this] in the plural form, because the aspect of evil is the aspect of separation, and it has no portion that belongs to one alone. Therefore, when Scripture mentions אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים, it mentions them in the plural designation." "<b>And gods of masking, etc.</b> It is difficult: did He not command in general terms concerning all elilim not to turn to them? What need is there to go back and command again about this? Further, why did He say <b>for yourselves</b>? Perhaps He intends what they of blessed memory said (Tikkunim 18), that when a person performs a worthy deed, the Shekhinah rests upon him, and when he transgresses the mouth of HaShem, the limb through which he sinned — a spirit of impurity rests upon it, and it is a mask (masekhah) that separates between him and his Elohim, as they said (Isaiah 59:2): ״עֲוֹנוֹתֵיכֶם מַבְדִּילִים״ (your iniquities have separated), and so on. And that is the meaning here of <b>gods of masking</b> — meaning, gods that are masks separating you from the source of life — do not make them for yourselves. From this you have learned that one who turns to idolatrous worship causes a mask that separates between him and his Maker, and therefore He concluded by saying <b>I am YHWH your Elohim</b>, to say from whom he is separated by reason of that mask." "<b>Further,</b> He may intend by His saying ״I am YHWH your Elohim״ that by guarding oneself from this prohibition of idolatrous worship, through this [one declares that] YHWH is his Elohim — in the way they said (Ḥullin 5a): whoever denies idolatrous worship is as one who affirms the entire Torah." "And by way of remez (allusion), He may intend in the manner they of blessed memory said (Megillah 28a) concerning the verse ״לוּלֵי פְּנֵי יְהוֹשָׁפָט״ and so on (II Kings 3:14) — by implication, it is forbidden to gaze upon the face of a wicked person. And that is the meaning here of <b>gods of masking you shall not make for yourselves</b> — meaning, you shall not make yourselves gods of masking such that it is forbidden to gaze upon you."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:58:53.446736021Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}